WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.646
There’s this coaster in Hershey Park Pennsylvania,
called the Sooperdooperlooper.

00:00:04.646 --> 00:00:08.550
On it, you come around this bend that’s
full of trees blocking your view

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.215
and before you know it 
you’re hurtling down the track

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:15.370
at 45 miles per hour toward 
a thrilling 75 foot loop.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.847
According to Hershey's website,

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:19.929
it’s the “first-ever looping roller coaster 
on the east coast”.

00:00:19.929 --> 00:00:23.005
But the good people at the Hershey company
have that wrong.

00:00:23.005 --> 00:00:26.015
Because about 75 years before the Sooperdooperlooper,

00:00:26.015 --> 00:00:28.999
Coney Island — in my hometown of New York —
was home to

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:32.054
the first two looping coasters in America.

00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:35.880
Unlike the Sooperdooperlooper, which has been
around for almost 50 years,

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.124
these early looping coasters didn’t even
last a decade.

00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:42.954
Because, it turns out, loops shouldn’t be
made like this.

00:00:46.411 --> 00:00:50.508
Looping roller coasters got their start in
Europe as early as 1842.

00:00:50.508 --> 00:00:52.836
Back then, they were called centrifugal railways

00:00:53.643 --> 00:00:56.780
They... weren’t super popular or successful.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:04.346
They tried a few more in Europe, as we understand
it, these were short lived and more a novelty.

00:01:04.346 --> 00:01:07.420
That’s Randy Geisler, former president and
current member of

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.280
the historical committee for 
American Coaster Enthusiasts.

00:01:10.885 --> 00:01:13.962
I've loved roller coasters my entire life.

00:01:13.962 --> 00:01:19.205
The movements, the speed, and just the world
whipping by you in every direction.

00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:24.187
People in the 1800s didn't have the same fondness
for looping coasters.

00:01:24.187 --> 00:01:29.413
And that didn’t change much when America
built its first looping roller coaster in 1895:

00:01:29.413 --> 00:01:31.896
Coney Island’s Flip Flap Railway.

00:01:31.896 --> 00:01:35.060
That’s because these coasters had a few
major problems.

00:01:35.535 --> 00:01:38.890
The biggest one being, they made the loops
circular.

00:01:40.345 --> 00:01:42.771
In physics, we love perfect circles.

00:01:42.771 --> 00:01:43.757
It's an easy shape.

00:01:43.757 --> 00:01:48.479
But from a human experience point of view,
it is a nightmare.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:49.739
That’s Matt Anderson.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.090
I’m a physics professor at 
San Diego State University.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:55.830
To make it all the way around
the Flip Flap’s circular loop

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.420
cars needed to move really, really fast
while changing directions.

00:01:59.856 --> 00:02:04.850
The coaster itself is trying to push you up
into a new direction.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.229
Your head, your spine, everything else wants
to keep going in a straight line.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.285
But the coaster is saying, no, you gotta curve
into this arc.

00:02:12.285 --> 00:02:16.640
Changing directions so rapidly causes a spike
in G forces/

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.288
You've gone from one G instantly 
to 12 or 14 Gs.

00:02:21.288 --> 00:02:22.565
That’s a big problem.

00:02:22.565 --> 00:02:26.330
6Gs is really the most a human body can handle
on a rollercoaster

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.342
and only for about a second.

00:02:28.342 --> 00:02:29.424
At that force...

00:02:29.424 --> 00:02:33.270
Literally, all the blood is just being pulled
out of your head

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.220
and you will pass out.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.670
You can see how wild the G-forces are in this
looping-coaster-simulation chart

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:45.556
created by George Sidebotham, a mechanical
engineering professor at the Cooper Union.

00:02:45.556 --> 00:02:49.790
Because of our chat, he became so obsessed
with the circular loop problem

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.586
that he made a bunch of looping-coaster-simulation
charts.

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:57.090
This line represents a circular loop like
the Flip Flap's.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.930
After jumping from 1 to 14 Gs, it would decrease
as you reached the top of the loop

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.530
then ramp all the way up again to 14 at the
bottom,

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.695
and then back to 1 as the coaster 
leaves the curve.

00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:11.125
Passengers were completely rattled.

00:03:11.125 --> 00:03:14.922
The Flip Flap, being made mostly of wood,
didn’t help either.

00:03:14.922 --> 00:03:17.100
Wood is stretchy and compress-y.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.360
You can see the circle moving as the cart
goes around.

00:03:21.735 --> 00:03:23.890
It's stressing it this way and then it's stressing
it that way.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:29.238
It just looks like an engineering catastrophe
about to happen.

00:03:29.238 --> 00:03:32.484
The next looping coaster built on Coney Island,
the Loop the Loop,

00:03:32.484 --> 00:03:34.900
Was crafted to avoid all those issues.

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:38.770
It was made entirely out of steel, which offered
more stability.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:42.050
And the circle was switched to more of an
upside down teardrop shape.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:46.570
Squeezing the sides in helped ease the transition
into and out of the curvature

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.960
reducing the G forces and creating a
smoother, less body-damaging ride.

00:03:51.291 --> 00:03:54.568
But still, they had the capacity problem.

00:03:54.568 --> 00:03:57.190
Neither the Flip Flap or the Loop the Loop
could support

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.005
more than a few riders at a time

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:02.640
because the loops were small, and so
the cars had to be small, too.

00:04:03.043 --> 00:04:05.680
Most of the cars, they seated 4 people

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.728
And pretty much you could only send people 
about once every 5 minutes.

00:04:09.728 --> 00:04:11.560
They just couldn't make enough money.

00:04:11.978 --> 00:04:15.010
There were more people that would watch
than ride.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.404
The Flip Flap lasted 7 years, 
the Loop the Loop lasted 9.

00:04:19.404 --> 00:04:22.210
And all other looping coasters 
around the world

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.254
shut down shortly after.

00:04:24.254 --> 00:04:26.929
It seemed like the end of the track for loops.

00:04:26.929 --> 00:04:30.442
That is... until something new came along.

00:04:30.442 --> 00:04:33.582
In 1959, Disney unveiled the Matterhorn.

00:04:33.582 --> 00:04:37.649
It didn’t have a loop but it was the very
first tubular steel coaster.

00:04:37.649 --> 00:04:40.440
Unlike regular steel which was solid throughout,

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.400
tubular steel was hollow in the middle, 
making it lighter-weight,

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.190
and easier to bend into smooth arcs and curves.

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:50.477
Unlike wood, tubular steel could support heavy
weight, and more dynamic movement.

00:04:50.477 --> 00:04:54.610
And in 1976, Six Flags Magic Mountain 
debuted the first

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.640
modern vertical looping coaster, 
made with tubular steel.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.680
The Great American Revolution squeezed the
loop even more

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:02.780
than the Loop the Loop

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.127
creating what’s known as a “clothoid loop".

00:05:05.127 --> 00:05:08.840
It gets smaller and smaller as you get to
the top

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.440
to try and even out the G forces.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.214
And then it does exact opposite as it comes
back down.

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:19.600
With clothoid loop-shaped coasters, designers
could distribute the G’s more evenly

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.900
and decide more precisely how many Gs to hit...

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:25.460
about 4.9 in the case of the Great American
Revolution.

00:05:26.022 --> 00:05:30.210
A much smoother experience compared to the
Flip Flap's uneven G force spikes

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:33.126
from 1 to 14 to 9 to 1.

00:05:33.126 --> 00:05:37.639
This clothoid loop — crafted using strong
and easy-to-bend tubular steel,

00:05:37.639 --> 00:05:40.110
and built 113 feet high

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:43.880
meant that the Great American Revolution 
could support 20 riders

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:47.412
at g forces that feel exhilarating instead
of harmful.

00:05:47.412 --> 00:05:51.090
And since then loops have exploded in popularity.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.090
Double loops!

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:53.090
Crazy loops!

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:55.457
And that Sooperdooperlooper 
in Hershey Park

00:05:55.457 --> 00:05:58.038
which, by the way, can proudly claim that

00:05:58.038 --> 00:06:02.246
it’s the first modern looping roller coaster 
on the east coast.

